ariable.\n\nMore importantly: $0770 stayed 0 and $0776 stayed 0 the whole time, and the screen didn't change. So the Start press didn't start the game. But $06FC read 1...\n\nHmm, what if the watch's \"1\" is actually the value at $06FC but the Start bit... no, 1 is Right.\n\nWait — could the game be in attract-demo mode where a demo is playing (auto Right)? The screen shows the title, but maybe... no.\n\nHold on. Let me reconsider: maybe the title screen we're seeing is actually the ATTRACT DEMO's title? And $0770=0 = title/attract. The demo plays after the title; during the demo, $06FC (the controller buffer) is written by the demo's synthetic input? No, the demo writes to the controller variable directly maybe.\n\nHmm, actually in SMB the attract demo feeds fake buttons into $06FC! Yes — the demo mode writes to the controller variable. That would explain $06FC=1 (Right) during the demo. But the screen shows the title, and the demo would show gameplay.\n\nHmm, confusing. Let me step back and check: maybe the emulator's framebuffer is stale? The screenshot sha identical across multiple calls... Earlier between title screenshots the sha was identical (2b9cfcd) even after 174 frames. And $0086=40, $071C=0, $0776=0, $0770=0.\n\nLet me take a fresh screenshot now after the 128-frame run.\n\nActually, maybe the game is at the title and just doesn't respond to Start because it's waiting for a \"demo counter\" or something. In SMB, the title screen waits; pressing Start starts the game. Should work.\n\nPossibility: the emulator's controller input isn't reaching the game because the bridge/play_input targets the wrong port? But earlier Start presses worked (we got from TIME UP into 1-1). So input works.\n\nWait, did they? Sequence: screenshot showed TIME UP (mode 1). Then I ran Start,wait,Start → got 1-1 TIME 398 paused. That's consistent with: the TIME UP sequence finished on its own (death), Mario respawned at 1-1 start, and my second Start paused it. Actually maybe my Start presses did nothing and the game just went through its own respawn: TIME UP → lose life → WORLD card → 1-1 restart (unpaused) → then my second Start paused it. Plausible!\n\nThen from the paused 1-1, I pressed Start (unpause) → ran 140 frames right → died (Goomba) → game over? → title. Hmm.\n\nThen from the title, Start didn't work.\n\nHmm. Maybe the Start isn't registering because play_input's \"start\" button runs at 8 frames but the game's joypad polling needs the button held at a particular time? Unlikely.\n\nLet me check $06FC mapping claim by a direct experiment: play_input holding 'start' and watching $06FC per frame. Actually earlier mapping says a per-frame watch caught Start pulses as 0x10 on frame 14. Let me replicate with sample_every 1.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the watch output: maybe I misread which samples. The values array has 43 entries for frames 3..128. 1s appear at indices 3..19. Index 3 = frame 3*3? The sample_frames list says [3,6,9,...]. So index 3 → frame 12. Hmm, so $06FC=1 from frame ~12 to ~57. That's the whole first segment where nothing was pressed!\n\nUnless the watch is reporting values in a shifted way. Whatever.\n\nLet me do a cleaner test: sample_every 1, script with distinct buttons, watch $06FC. That'll tell me the encoding. But that's a side quest.\n\nMore important: why isn't Start starting the game? Let me look at the screen now.\n\nActually! Maybe the issue: the title screen requires pressing Start, but the game only reads Start on the title if \"number of players\" cursor... no.\n\nLet me check $075A (lives). If game over happened, lives could be... Let me read $075A, $0770, and take a screenshot.\n\nAlso possible: the title screen shown is actually after game over, and pressing Start does start, but the transition takes >170 frames? SMB title → Start → WORLD 1-1 card (~2.5 s = 150 frames) → level. 170 frames should be enough.\n\nHmm, unless the Start press wasn't registered because the title screen only polls Star