y's own handler using $0087... hmm.\n\nOK here's another thought: maybe SMB's object arrays index X = 0 for the PLAYER and X = 1..6 for enemies? Hmm, then the Goomba is X=1 and $0087.\n\nAlternatively X = 0..5 for enemies and the player uses X=0 too (they're separate calls)? The routine $F171 reads $0086,X for whatever X. The trace showed two different Y values (0 and 1) written; the X used could be the same for both calls? No, if X were 0 for both, $03AD and $03AE would be the same value. Samples: frame0: $03AD=46, $03AE=143. Different → different objects → X=0 and X=1.\n\nSo object 0 (player) x = $0086, object 1 x = $0087. And object 1 IS being processed as an object. The Goomba should be object 1.\n\nSo why didn't the sprite move? Unless the sprite I saw was object 1 but at a position computed with the PAGE: screen x = (page*256 + x_low) - (scroll_page*256 + scroll). With enemy page=1 and scroll page=0: screen x = (256+240) - 116 = 380 → off-screen. Then the engine's \"keep enemy on screen\" logic might clamp or the enemy is drawn off-screen (not in OAM). But we saw a Goomba at 174 — which would be object 1's position if x_low=34.\n\nHmm, so maybe the game RESTORED $0087 to 34 before drawing? But read showed 239.\n\nUnless... the sprite at 174 belongs to a SECOND enemy (object 2), whose x is NOT in the $0086 array... but $0088=128. Hmm.\n\nHmm, hold on, maybe the Goomba's x is at $0087 and additionally the game keeps a separate \"enemy x screen\" in the OAM built during the frame; and my write of 240 caused the enemy to be flagged for erase and its OAM entry was built from the OLD value because the erase happens after the draw. Eh.\n\nLet me just re-run a cleaner x write test: write $0087 to a value that keeps the enemy on screen, and screenshot BEFORE and AFTER with the game paused and stepping 1 frame; also list_sprites. If the Goomba moves, proven.\n\nBut careful: the enemy has a page at $006E=1. Level x = 256 + $0087. To move the Goomba to screen x ~200, I ne