nd $006E read 1 (I only read it before the write; let me read after).\n\nActually, in the LAST test I read $006E before the write only (109-110 = [0,1]) and not after. Let me read $006E now.\n\nHmm, I already restored state 2 and did the write. $006E now? Let me read $006E and $0087 etc. Also note the screenshot sha is identical to the previous test's screenshot — which means the whole framebuffer is identical, including the Goomba at 174!\n\nWait a moment. The screenshot sha 9430187347022180f82fdd82539f683ba2178e66b35a3db95518cc4261ae837d is the SAME as the screenshot I took after the earlier write of $0087=240 (which also showed Goomba at 174). But the two tests were at different times with different prior states (the first was after walking left, $0087=4; this one after load_state 2, $0087=34). Both screenshots are pixel-identical?! That's suspicious.\n\nThe earlier write test: after writing 240 and stepping, screenshot sha 943018...\nNow: after writing 100 and stepping, screenshot sha 943018... identical.\n\nBoth show Mario at 112 and Goomba at 174, TIME 346. But in the first test, Mario was at 228 level (screen 112) and camera 116; in this test, load_state 2 → Mario 228, camera 116. Same! And both wrote $0087 and stepped 1 frame. The framebuffer after 1 frame could be identical if the Goomba didn't move and the write didn't affect the drawing. And TIME 346 in both.\n\nOK so consistent. The Goomba at 174 is unaffected by $0087.\n\nHmm, so I'm now doubting that $0087 is the Goomba's x. But the numbers align perfectly (page 1, low 34 = level 290 = screen 174).\n\nOH WAIT!!! Maybe the Goomba is at slot 1, but the OAM/drawing uses $03AE which is computed... and my write DID change $03AE to 240, but the drawn sprite at 174 is at slot 1's position from the PREVIOUS frame's OAM? No, step_frame redraws.\n\nHmm, alternatively: maybe the sprite drawing for enemies uses the \"Enemy_X_Position\" array but ALSO clamps/erases off-screen. With low=99 (level 355, screen 239), it's on-screen (239<256). Should draw.\n\nUnless the enemy's drawing uses the PAGE: (1*256 + 99) - camera 116 = 239. Still on-screen.\n\nI'm stuck on reconciling. Let me do a decisive experiment: instead of writing $0087, write $0086 (player) — known to move Mario. That validates my method. Then...","truncated":true}
